1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"library--domains","kind":"library-article","route":"/roadmap/library/domains","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/library/domains","sourceHash":"9b57788ef78fdd8c6f8dd9fba408c158b8a5853a1350e91dffacbdce6fe994e5","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T16:52:50.281Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"Domains","zh":"领域"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"A domain is a category of language or content that shares vocabulary, style, and subject matter. A medical drama is a different domain than a cooking show, which is a different domain than a fantasy novel, which is a different domain than a casual conversation between friends.","zh":"领域是指在词汇、风格和主题方面具有共性的语言或内容类别。医疗剧、烹饪节目、奇幻小说和朋友间的日常闲聊，分别属于不同的领域。"}},{"id":"why-domains-matter","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Why Domains Matter","zh":"为什么领域很重要"}},{"id":"segment-0003","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Beyond the most common 2000 words, the vocabulary you need depends fairly heavily on <em>what</em> you're consuming. The next 1000 most common words in a medical drama are completely different from the next 1000 in a sports podcast. If you spread your immersion across many different domains, you'll encounter lots of words but each one less often — which makes them harder to learn.","zh":"掌握最常见的 2000 个词之后，接下来需要哪些词汇，很大程度上取决于你在接触<em>什么内容</em>。医疗剧中接下来最常见的 1000 个词，与体育播客中的完全不同。如果把沉浸分散到许多不同领域，你会遇到大量词汇，但每个词出现的频率都会降低，因此更难学会。"}},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"By focusing on a smaller number of domains at first, you encounter the same specialized vocabulary repeatedly, which means you learn it faster. Research suggests that with focused domain immersion, you can reach 90%+ comprehension of a specific type of content with far fewer words than you'd need for broad comprehension across all content types.","zh":"初期专注于少数几个领域，可以让你反复遇到相同的专业词汇，从而更快掌握它们。研究表明，通过专注于特定领域的沉浸，你只需掌握远少于广泛理解各类内容所需的词汇量，就能对某一类内容达到 90% 以上的理解程度。"}},{"id":"how-to-use-domains","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"How to Use Domains","zh":"如何利用领域"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Phase 1-2:</strong> When you're still a beginner, the only domain that's <em>really</em> accessible to you will be \"Comprehensible Input\" and maybe childrens TV. That's perfectly fine to use in this phase. Or if you're the type of person who needs to use more interesting content, picking 1 easier domain is best.","zh":"<strong>阶段 1–2：</strong>当你还是初学者时，真正<em>适合</em>你的领域可能只有“可理解输入”，或许还包括儿童电视节目。在这个阶段使用这些内容完全没问题。如果你需要更有趣的内容才能坚持，最好选择一个难度较低的领域。"}},{"id":"segment-0007","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Phase 2B-2C:</strong> In this pahse, your goal is to","zh":"<strong>阶段 2B-2C：</strong> 在这一阶段，你的目标是"}},{"id":"segment-0008","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Phase 3+:</strong> As your level increases, you can broaden into more domains. Each new domain requires learning some new vocabulary, but the process gets faster because vocabulary starts to overlap a lot more.","zh":"<strong>阶段 3 及以后：</strong>随着水平提高，你可以逐渐拓展到更多领域。每进入一个新领域，都需要学习一些新词汇；但由于不同领域的词汇重叠会越来越多，这个过程也会越来越快。"}},{"id":"segment-0009","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"<strong>Phase 6-7:</strong> Deliberately seeking out unfamiliar domains is how you fill vocabulary gaps and round out your fluency.","zh":"<strong>阶段 6–7：</strong>主动接触不熟悉的领域，可以帮助你填补词汇空缺，让语言更加全面流利。"}},{"id":"choosing-your-domains","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Choosing Your Domains","zh":"选择你的领域"}},{"id":"segment-0011","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Pick content you genuinely enjoy. The best domain for learning is the one that makes you want to keep immersing. <strong>Some good starting domains</strong>:","zh":"选择你真正喜欢的内容。最适合学习的领域，就是能让你想继续沉浸下去的领域。<strong>适合入门的领域包括</strong>："}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"Slice-of-life TV shows (most common in Japanese, but occasionally exist in other languages)","zh":"日常生活类电视节目（在日语内容中最常见，但其他语言偶尔也有）"},{"en":"Dramas (basic plots usually focused around characters)","zh":"剧情剧（情节通常比较简单，并以人物为核心）"},{"en":"Serialized novelas (basically the same as dramas, but more over the top)","zh":"系列肥皂剧（基本上和剧情剧相似，但情节更加夸张）"},{"en":"YouTube vlogs (casual, conversational language)","zh":"YouTube 视频博客（使用轻松、口语化的语言）"},{"en":"Cooking shows/videos (tons of overlapping vocabulary and you can make the food at home!)","zh":"烹饪节目或视频（重复词汇非常多，而且你还能在家照着做！）"},{"en":"Anime/cartoons (the language is usually a bit stylized, but can still be great immersion material, especially if it's what you enjoy)","zh":"动漫或动画片（语言通常略显风格化，但仍然可以成为很好的沉浸材料，尤其是当你本来就喜欢这类内容时）"},{"en":"Documentaries (ideally around similar topics, ie nature)","zh":"纪录片（最好围绕相近的主题，例如自然）"},{"en":"Crime or fantasy or sci-fi novels","zh":"犯罪、奇幻或科幻小说"}]},{"id":"segment-0013","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"There are lots of things to choose from, so don't worry too much about making the perfect choice or sticking with this list. However, some good things to <em>avoid</em> as a beginner are domains like comedy (too reliant on cultural knowledge) and current events (also relies on culteral knowledge, plus uses more formal language that doesn't overlap as much with day to day conversation).","zh":"可选择的内容很多，所以不必过于担心自己是否做出了完美选择，也不必局限于这份清单。不过，初学者最好<em>避开</em>喜剧（过度依赖文化背景知识）和时事新闻（同样依赖文化背景知识，而且使用的语言更正式，与日常对话的重叠较少）等领域。"}},{"id":"domain-overlap","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Domain Overlap","zh":"领域间的重叠"}},{"id":"segment-0015","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Domains aren't isolated. A lot of vocabulary is shared across domains, and mastering one domain makes the next one easier, especially after the 3rd or 4th. The jump from domain 3 to domain 4 is <em>much</em> smaller than the jump to domain 1 from nothing.","zh":"各个领域并非彼此孤立。许多词汇会在不同领域中共用，掌握一个领域后，下一个领域就会更容易，尤其是在掌握第三或第四个领域之后。从第三个领域拓展到第四个领域，难度<em>远远</em>低于从零开始进入第一个领域。"}},{"id":"research-and-reasoning","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Research and Reasoning","zh":"研究与依据"}},{"id":"segment-0017","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Nation (2006) demonstrated that the vocabulary needed for comprehension varies significantly depending on the type of text. His analyses showed that a single novel requires far fewer word families for 98% coverage than a broad corpus of mixed texts, because vocabulary recycles more within a consistent genre. Sutarsyah, Nation, and Kennedy (1994) found a similar pattern when analyzing a single university economics textbook — learners needed roughly 5,000 word families to reach adequate coverage of that one domain, compared to the 8,000–9,000 needed for general written English.","zh":"Nation（2006）证明，达到理解所需的词汇量会因文本类型而有显著差异。他的分析显示，要达到 98% 的词汇覆盖率，阅读一部小说所需的词族数量远少于阅读由多种文本组成的广泛语料库，因为在同一体裁中，词汇会更频繁地重复出现。Sutarsyah、Nation 和 Kennedy（1994）在分析一本大学经济学教材时发现了类似规律：学习者只需掌握约 5000 个词族，就能充分理解这一个领域；而要理解一般英语书面语，则需要掌握 8000–9000 个词族。"}},{"id":"segment-0018","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Webb and Rodgers (2009) extended this finding to television, showing that different TV genres have distinct vocabulary profiles. The specialized vocabulary within a genre — the words beyond the most frequent 2,000–3,000 — concentrates heavily, meaning that sticking with one type of show produces far more repeated encounters with the same words than jumping between genres. This matters because repeated encounters are one of the strongest predictors of vocabulary acquisition. Webb (2007) found that the more times a learner encounters a word, the more dimensions of knowledge they acquire — not just recognition but also collocational and grammatical knowledge.","zh":"Webb 和 Rodgers（2009）将这一发现拓展到了电视内容，证明不同电视节目类型具有不同的词汇分布。某一类型中的专业词汇——即最常见的 2000–3000 个词以外的词汇——高度集中。这意味着，与不断切换节目类型相比，持续观看同一类节目会让你更频繁地重复遇到相同的词。重复接触是预测词汇习得效果最有力的指标之一，因此这一点十分重要。Webb（2007）发现，学习者遇到一个词的次数越多，对这个词掌握的层面就越丰富——不仅能认出它，还会逐渐掌握其搭配和语法知识。"}}],"cards":[]}}]
